---
import Legal from '../../../layouts/Legal.astro';
import { me } from '../../../data.js';

const title = 'GrammarLens privacy policy';
const description = 'What data GrammarLens collects, where it goes, and how long it is kept.';
const updated = '22 September 2026';
---

<Legal title={title} description={description} noindex>
  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>Privacy</strong>
      GrammarLens for iOS<br />
      Last updated: {updated}
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h1 class="display">Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        GrammarLens has no accounts, never asks for your email, and shows no ads. Your
        name, your learning goal, your avatar and your entire practice history —
        including the Daily Test — stay on your phone; the Daily Test is scored on the
        device and never leaves it. If you turn on AI feedback for Topic Practice, the
        answer you type and the question it responds to are sent through our server to
        Anthropic (Claude) so it can check your work — only after you've agreed to it
        in the app, and you can withdraw that agreement at any time.
      </p>

      <p class="links-label">Contents</p>
      <ul class="links toc-list">
        <li><a href="#who-is-responsible">Who is responsible</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-stays-on-your-device">What stays on your device</a></li>
        <li><a href="#the-daily-test">The Daily Test</a></li>
        <li><a href="#permission-for-ai-feedback">Permission for AI feedback (Topic Practice)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-our-server-keeps">What our server keeps</a></li>
        <li><a href="#anthropic-as-a-processor">Anthropic as a processor</a></li>
        <li><a href="#analytics-and-crash-reporting">Analytics and crash reporting</a></li>
        <li><a href="#subscriptions">Subscriptions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-we-never-send">What we never send</a></li>
        <li><a href="#keeping-and-deleting-data">Keeping and deleting data</a></li>
        <li><a href="#children">Children</a></li>
        <li><a href="#your-rights">Your rights</a></li>
        <li><a href="#changes">Changes</a></li>
        <li><a href="#contact">Contact</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>01</strong>
      Who is<br />
      responsible
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="who-is-responsible">Who is responsible</h2>
      <p>
        GrammarLens is made by {me.name}, Istanbul, Turkey. For anything in this
        policy, write to <a href={`mailto:${me.email}`}>{me.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>02</strong>
      What stays on<br />
      your device
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="what-stays-on-your-device">What stays on your device</h2>
      <p>
        Everything below is stored in the app's local database and is removed when you
        delete the app. None of it is ever sent anywhere:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your first name and your stated learning goal, from onboarding</li>
        <li>Your chosen avatar and your app settings, including text size and theme</li>
        <li>
          Your weak-spot profile — which grammar topics you get wrong and how often,
          worked out entirely on your device
        </li>
        <li>
          Every Daily Test question and answer — the Daily Test is scored on your
          device and never leaves it
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Your practice history for Topic Practice — the questions you're given and the
        answers you type — is also stored on your device. The one exception: if you've
        turned on AI feedback, the answers and matching questions from the session you
        submit are sent off-device for scoring; see <a href="#permission-for-ai-feedback">Permission for AI feedback</a> for exactly what that involves and how to control it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Separately, your text-size preference and totals about your sessions — like how
        many questions you answered or skipped — are sent to our analytics provider as
        numbers and category labels, never as text; see
        <a href="#analytics-and-crash-reporting">Analytics and crash reporting</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>03</strong>
      The Daily Test
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="the-daily-test">The Daily Test</h2>
      <p>
        The very first day's test is built into the app itself, so it needs no network
        request at all. On later days, the app asks our server for a new set — that
        request includes only the anonymous device identifier used for daily limits
        (see <a href="#what-our-server-keeps">What our server keeps</a>) and carries
        nothing else about you or your history; the identifier itself is never
        forwarded to Anthropic. Every Daily Test, on any day, is scored entirely on
        your device.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>04</strong>
      Permission for<br />
      AI feedback
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="permission-for-ai-feedback">Permission for AI feedback (Topic Practice)</h2>
      <p>
        Topic Practice — where GrammarLens checks what you write and explains your
        mistakes — is the only place your own words leave your device, and only once
        you say yes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Before you use it for the first time, the app shows a permission screen that
        states what is sent (the answer you type, together with the question it
        responds to), who receives it (Anthropic, the company that makes Claude,
        reached through our own server), and what is never sent (your name, your
        learning goal or your avatar — Daily Test answers stay on your device
        regardless). You can decline and still use the Daily Test.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can review or withdraw this permission at any time in Profile → Data.
        Turning it off takes effect immediately; Topic Practice will ask again the next
        time you use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Please don't type personal details — such as your full name, address or
        contact information — into your answers, since we can't screen what you write
        before it's sent.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>05</strong>
      What our<br />
      server keeps
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="what-our-server-keeps">What our server keeps</h2>
      <p>
        Our server (a Cloudflare Worker) never stores the text of your questions or
        answers — it passes each request to Anthropic and returns the result. It keeps
        three things, briefly, as operational records only:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Usage counters.</strong> A random, anonymous identifier — created by the app on
          your device, not your device's serial number, advertising ID, or any other
          hardware or system value — is used only to enforce a daily limit on how many
          exercises one installation can generate, which is what keeps the service
          affordable to run. Limits are scoped to the calendar day (UTC) and counters
          delete themselves automatically 48 hours after they are last written. This
          identifier is <strong>never sent to Anthropic</strong>. Deleting and reinstalling the
          app produces a new identifier.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A request log, for every completed call.</strong> Which kind of operation it was, how
          many questions it covered, how many tokens were used, and how long it took —
          never the questions or answers themselves.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Error logs.</strong> When something goes wrong — the AI service is unreachable, or
          returns something the app cannot read — our server records the failure so the
          problem can be found and fixed. These records do not contain the text you
          wrote either.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Both logs are kept only as long as our hosting provider, Cloudflare, retains
        Workers log output on our current (free) plan — about three days — and are
        then deleted.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>06</strong>
      Anthropic as<br />
      a processor
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="anthropic-as-a-processor">Anthropic as a processor</h2>
      <p>
        When you use Topic Practice with permission on, the answer you typed and the
        matching question are processed by Anthropic to generate your feedback. How
        Anthropic retains that data, and whether it is used for training, is governed
        by Anthropic's own terms and policies, not ours to restate here. See
        <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/legal/commercial-terms">Anthropic's Commercial Terms of Service</a>
        and <a href="https://privacy.claude.com/en/articles/7996868-is-my-data-used-for-model-training">Anthropic's explanation of API data and training</a>
        for the current terms.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>07</strong>
      Analytics and<br />
      crash reporting
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="analytics-and-crash-reporting">Analytics and crash reporting</h2>
      <p>
        GrammarLens uses Google's Firebase Analytics and Firebase Crashlytics to
        understand how the app is used and to find crashes.
      </p>
      <p><strong>What we send deliberately</strong> falls into a few categories:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Which screens and modes you open</li>
        <li>
          How practice sessions and the Daily Test go — like how many questions were
          answered or skipped
        </li>
        <li>Progress milestones, such as medals</li>
        <li>Steps through the paywall and purchase flow</li>
        <li>Your text-size preference</li>
        <li>Your AI-feedback permission decision (see <a href="#permission-for-ai-feedback">Permission for AI feedback</a>)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The exact events inside these categories may change as the app evolves; what
        doesn't change is the boundary — none of them ever carry your name, your
        learning goal, or the text of any question or answer.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What Firebase collects automatically.</strong> Independently of the events above,
        Firebase records a randomly generated app instance identifier, your device model
        and operating system version, your app version, your approximate region and
        language, and session counts and durations. Crashlytics additionally records
        technical details about a crash when one happens — device and app information,
        the error message, and where in the app it occurred.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is not linked to your identity. GrammarLens does not use an advertising
        identifier (IDFA), and we do not use this data to track you across other apps or
        websites. If you would rather not send this, iOS settings let you limit app
        tracking and diagnostics at the system level.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>08</strong>
      Subscriptions
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="subscriptions">Subscriptions</h2>
      <p>
        GrammarLens Premium is sold through Apple's App Store. <strong>We never see your
        payment details</strong> — your card, your Apple ID and your billing address stay with
        Apple.
      </p>
      <p>
        To know whether your subscription is active, the app uses RevenueCat, a
        subscription management service. RevenueCat receives an anonymous app user
        identifier, the purchase and renewal status of your subscription, and basic
        device and operating system information. It does not receive your name, your
        answers, or your practice history.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>09</strong>
      What we<br />
      never send
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="what-we-never-send">What we never send</h2>
      <p>Regardless of feature or permission state, GrammarLens never sends:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your name, your learning goal or your avatar</li>
        <li>
          Your stored practice history (other than the Topic Practice answers you
          choose to submit for feedback — see <a href="#permission-for-ai-feedback">Permission for AI feedback</a>)
        </li>
        <li>
          Daily Test questions or answers — the Daily Test is scored entirely on your
          device
        </li>
        <li>Your contacts, your precise location, or any advertising identifier</li>
        <li>Your payment details — Apple handles those directly</li>
      </ul>
      <p>And more generally:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>No advertising, and no advertising identifiers</li>
        <li>No selling or sharing of personal data with anyone</li>
        <li>No tracking of you across other apps or websites</li>
        <li>No account, no password, no email address required to use the app</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>10</strong>
      Keeping and<br />
      deleting data
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="keeping-and-deleting-data">Keeping and deleting data</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Everything stored on your device is deleted when you delete the app.</li>
        <li>Usage counters on our server delete themselves after 48 hours.</li>
        <li>Request and error logs are kept for about three days by Cloudflare.</li>
        <li>
          We do not hold a copy of your practice history, so there is nothing for us to
          send you or delete on request.
        </li>
        <li>
          Analytics and crash data are held by Google under Firebase's own retention
          settings.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>11</strong>
      Children
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="children">Children</h2>
      <p>
        GrammarLens is intended for users aged 13 or over, or the minimum age required
        in your country, whichever is higher. We do not knowingly collect personal data
        from anyone younger.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>12</strong>
      Your rights
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="your-rights">Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        Depending on where you live, you may have the right to access, correct, delete
        or export your personal data, to object to processing, or to complain to a
        supervisory authority. Because the app holds your practice data on your own
        device and we do not maintain an account for you, most of these rights you can
        exercise directly — deleting the app deletes the data. For anything else, write
        to <a href={`mailto:${me.email}`}>{me.email}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>13</strong>
      Changes
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="changes">Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes in a way that affects what we do with your data, we will
        update the date at the top of this page and, where the change is significant,
        note it in the app.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="row">
    <aside class="note">
      <strong>14</strong>
      Contact
    </aside>
    <div class="body">
      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        <a href={`mailto:${me.email}`}>{me.email}</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/products/grammarlens/privacy">Privacy Policy</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
        <a href="/products/grammarlens/terms">Terms of Service</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
        <a href="/products/grammarlens/support">Support</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Legal>

<style>
  /* The contents list: each <li> holds nothing but its link, so the link
     itself can be the full-height tappable row (including what was the
     <li>'s own padding) instead of just the text's own line box. */
  .toc-list li {
    padding: 0;
  }

  .toc-list a {
    display: block;
    padding: 12px 0;
  }
</style>
